= Reproducibility — Technical =

== Computational state ==
A computational result can depend on a state vector such as:

<pre>
R = F(D, C, P, E, H, S)
</pre>

where:
* <code>D</code> = data;
* <code>C</code> = code;
* <code>P</code> = parameters/configuration;
* <code>E</code> = software environment/dependencies;
* <code>H</code> = hardware/runtime behavior;
* <code>S</code> = stochastic state/random seeds.

Reproducibility requires controlling or recording every component that materially affects R.

== Exact and tolerance-based reproducibility ==
Exact reproducibility requires identical output.

Numerical/scientific reproducibility may instead define:

<pre>
distance(R1, R2) ≤ ε
</pre>

or require the same inferential conclusion under accepted numerical tolerance.

The equivalence relation must be part of the specification.

== Environment capture ==
Environment capture can use:
* containers;
* Nix/Guix-like declarative environments;
* lockfiles;
* immutable package snapshots;
* virtual machines;
* software-bill-of-materials records.

Containers do not fully capture kernel, CPU, GPU, firmware, external services, or nondeterministic scheduling.

== Workflow provenance ==
A reproducible pipeline needs dependency structure:

<pre>
input artifact hashes
→ transformation versions
→ parameters
→ output artifact hashes
</pre>

Workflow DAGs plus content hashes can make omitted or stale intermediate products visible.

== Randomness ==
For stochastic algorithms, setting a seed can reproduce one pseudorandom sequence, but scientific reproducibility may require demonstrating stable conclusions across seeds rather than reusing one convenient realization.

Parallel random-number generation requires explicit stream/substream control.

== Floating point ==
Floating-point addition is not associative:

<pre>
(a + b) + c ≠ a + (b + c)
</pre>

in finite precision.

Parallel execution can therefore change low-order bits through different reduction order.

Bitwise reproducibility may require deterministic algorithms, fixed libraries, controlled hardware, or compensated arithmetic.

== Reproducibility as verification ==
Computational reproduction verifies that a documented execution path can regenerate the reported artifact.

It does not validate the underlying model, data collection, causal inference, or scientific interpretation.

